## 1. Objective

The objective of this peer-assisted demonstration was to review essential Cloud Computing and DevOps development workflows with a fellow student requiring additional guidance on earlier course assignments.

The exercise focused on demonstrating practical understanding of SSH authentication, Git Bash, Git version control, Visual Studio Code, and Cline-based LLM assistance.

The session also provided an opportunity to explain how these tools support the shared Cloudmesh framework and its development workflow.

## 2. Development Environment

The demonstrated development environment included:

- Windows and Git Bash
- Visual Studio Code
- Git and GitHub
- SSH and SSH-agent functionality
- Cloudmesh course repositories
- Cline integrated into VS Code
- An AI model configured for development assistance

## 3. Demonstration Activities

### 3.1 SSH and Authentication

The walkthrough covered SSH identity management and GitHub authentication.

The distinction between SSH private keys, public keys, key fingerprints, and identities loaded into the SSH agent was explained.

Relevant commands included `ssh-add -l` and `ssh -T git@github.com`.

### 3.2 Git Bash and Repository Management

The demonstration covered navigating repository directories, identifying the active Git branch, inspecting working-tree status, and identifying remote repositories.

Commands included `git status -sb`, `git remote -v`, and `code .`.

The relationship between local repositories, personal forks, upstream repositories, commits, and pull requests was discussed.

### 3.3 Cline and AI-Assisted Development

The walkthrough demonstrated the integration of Cline into Visual Studio Code.

The role of an LLM in source-code explanation, debugging, and development planning was reviewed.

Particular emphasis was placed on independently verifying AI-generated recommendations against source code and test results.

### 3.4 Practical Verification

The session was designed to reinforce practical understanding through observable command execution and explanation.

The actual independently completed tasks and their results will be shared as demonstrable screenshot images.

